csv"], ["Grep", "events\\.csv"], ["Bash", "cat README.md; echo ---; cat -A events.csv"]], "final_message": "The likely cause is that the timestamps in `events.csv` are in three different formats, so they get read as different timezones. I only have the 3-row file and no charting code, so I haven't reproduced the jumps in a chart.\n\n```\nid,ts\n1,2026-03-01T10:00:00Z          \u2190 UTC (explicit \"Z\")\n2,2026-03-01T10:05:00           \u2190 no timezone at all (naive)\n3,2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00     \u2190 UTC-6 offset (= 10:10Z)\n```\n\n- **Row 1 and row 3 are unambiguous.** They are 10:00Z and 10:10Z, so they fall 10 minutes apart in UTC.\n- **Row 2 has no timezone.** Most tools guess here, and the guess is what moves events by hours:\n  - pandas, JavaScript and similar tools treat a naive timestamp as local time or as UTC, depending on the library and its settings. If the chart is built on a machine that isn't on UTC, row 2 moves by that machine's offset.\n  - If a tool drops the offset on row 3 instead of converting it, that row reads as 04:10 and lands 6 hours early. Some parsers and spreadsheet imports do this.\n- **The size of the jump depends on who builds the chart.** Different timezones on different machines would explain the \"several hours\" you see.\n\n**Fix**\n1. Parse everything as UTC, with something like `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)`. Don't rely on the default behavior.\n2. Decide what the naive timestamps mean. If you know the source system writes UTC, localize them to UTC explicitly. I can't tell from the file alone.\n3. Better, fix the exporter to always write an explicit offset or `Z`.\n\nIf you share the chart script, or tell me which tool builds the charts, I can check which of these is happening. Row 2's source system is the one thing I'd confirm first.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}